{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# J5 — A Dockerized Jira harness as the Data Center verification oracle\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nThere is no way to buy a Jira Data Center license to test against: Atlassian ended new DC purchases on\n30 March 2026 and no longer issues DC trial licenses. There is also **no official OpenAPI/Swagger spec for Jira\nDC** — Atlassian publishes only WADL, and the long-standing requests for one (JRASERVER-65954, JRASERVER-68539)\nremain open — so spec-driven contract testing is unavailable. Cloud's v3 swagger is not a substitute: different\nauth, ADF vs wiki markup, accountId vs name. Without an oracle, a DC adapter is unverifiable except against\nfakes that encode our own assumptions.\n\n## Approach\n\nUse the **purpose-built test image the pycontribs/jira project uses for exactly this problem** — a thin wrapper\nover `addono/jira-software-standalone` that stands up a real Jira 8.17.1 at `http://localhost:2990/jira` with\ndefault credentials `admin`/`admin`. It is unaffected by the DC trial shutdown, and it is the substrate the most\nwidely used Python Jira client validates itself against. Jira 8.17.1 is **≥ 8.14**, the release that introduced\nPersonal Access Tokens, so the PAT bearer auth the DC transport uses is exercised for real.\n\n**The image must be BUILT, not pulled — corrected against the registry, not assumed.** An earlier draft of this\nplan said to `docker run pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1` and called it \"a pinned, pullable image\". That is\nfalse and was verified false before this plan was rewritten:\n\n- `docker pull pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1` → `pull access denied ... repository does not exist`.\n- The Docker Hub API for `pycontribs/jira-test-image` returns `{\"message\": \"object not found\"}`. The\n  `pycontribs` Hub namespace contains exactly seven repositories, all OS base images (centos, fedora, ubuntu,\n  debian, alpine, opensuse, voidlinux). There is no `jira-test-image`.\n- That tag is a **local build name**. `pycontribs/jira` carries `docker/jira-test-image/{Dockerfile,README.md}`,\n  and the upstream README's own instruction is\n  `docker build -t pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1 docker/jira-test-image`. It is published to no registry.\n- Its base, `addono/jira-software-standalone`, **is** pullable, and the exact digest pycontribs pins\n  (`sha256:04326628dc4ac36b2bfc1d0f2ebe5ba3807c1ec9cf9b18307d3c2ad7222537e9`) resolves.\n\nFeasibility was proven before this rewrite: the upstream Dockerfile was fetched and built on the target host\n(879 MB image, container starts and stays up). So the oracle is real; only the acquisition mechanism changes.\n\n**Decision: vendor a ~15-line Dockerfile in `tests/external/live_jira_dc/`** that `FROM`s the addono base **by digest**\nand reproduces the two things the upstream wrapper adds, with attribution to pycontribs/jira in a comment.\nRationale: building from a third-party repo at test time would make the harness depend on another project's\ndirectory layout and default branch staying put; vendoring the few lines is self-documenting and pins our own\nsupply chain. The alternative — a `git clone` of pycontribs/jira in the fixture — is rejected for that reason.\n\n**Three details are load-bearing and must survive into the vendored file as comments**, because each looks like\nremovable noise and each breaks the harness if removed:\n\n1. **`-DskipAllPrompts=true` on the `atlas-run` ENTRYPOINT is MANDATORY.** Atlassian shut down the Marketplace\n   v1 REST endpoint that AMPS hardcodes for its SDK update check, so without this flag **every cold container\n   start** fails with `FileNotFoundException` on `https://marketplace.atlassian.com/rest/1.0/plugins/...`. The\n   failure looks like a transient network fault, so a maintainer \"tidying\" the flag away would produce a\n   confusing, seemingly-flaky breakage.\n2. **`-dit` (equivalently compose's `tty: true` + `stdin_open: true`) is REQUIRED** — AMPS exits without a\n   controlling TTY. A plain compose service block is not sufficient.\n3. **Only Jira 8.x is supported.** `JIRA_VERSION` is overridable across 8.x patch versions, but 9+/11+ fail\n   Maven resolution because the upstream pom is anchored to the 8.x dependency graph. The README must say so,\n   or a future reader will assume the harness can track a client's newer Jira by bumping a variable.\n\n**The image does NOT contain Jira — `atlas-run` downloads it at runtime.** Verified by running it: the\ncontainer log shows `Configured Artifact: com.atlassian.jira.plugins:jira-plugin-test-resources:8.17.1:zip`\nfollowed by ~917 `Downloading from atlassian-public: https://maven.atlassian.com/...` lines before Tomcat and\nH2 come up. Four consequences the harness must design around:\n\n- **Egress to `maven.atlassian.com` is a hard RUNTIME dependency**, not a one-time image pull. An\n  egress-restricted CI runner cannot start this harness at all.\n- **`pinning the image digest does NOT pin Jira itself`.** If Atlassian withdraws the 8.17.1 artifacts from\n  their public Maven repo, the harness stops working despite our pinned base digest. That, not the image, is\n  the real fragility, and the README must say so — it also strengthens the case for the separately-tracked\n  real-instance validation (`9895-ac7d-4276-4f08`).\n- First start is dominated by that download, so the readiness budget must cover a large artifact fetch, not\n  just JVM boot.\n- The fixture SHOULD mount a persistent Maven cache (`~/.m2`) as a volume, or every fresh container\n  re-downloads ~900 artifacts.\n\n**No licensing claim is made.** An earlier draft said the image \"carries its own license\". That is a legal\ncharacterization this story has not verified, so it is replaced by the observed behaviour: no license key is\nsupplied to build or start the instance, and the Atlassian SDK's `atlas-run` provisions a local developer\ninstance. The README states it that way.\n\n**Local runs on Apple Silicon are the constrained path, and were measured.** Three attempts on an arm64\nworkstation failed: two killed by Cargo's default 600000 ms deploy ceiling, and a third — with\n`-Dproduct.start.timeout` correctly lifting that ceiling — still never reached Jira startup in 55 minutes. Two\ncompounding causes, both recorded so a future reader does not repeat the runs: (a) the amd64 base under arm64\n**emulation**, and (b) **resource starvation** — the colima VM was 2 vCPU / 4 GiB with seven other containers\nalready resident, against a Jira that wants ~2 GB of heap by itself. A local run therefore needs BOTH a larger,\nRosetta-backed VM (e.g. `colima start --cpu 6 --memory 12 --vm-type vz --vz-rosetta`) AND spare capacity; since\nthat restarts the VM and evicts every running container, it is an operator decision, not something the harness\ncan assume. Prefer the CI job.\n\n**Platform is a real variable, not a footnote.** The base image is `linux/amd64`. On an `arm64` host (Apple\nSilicon, ARM CI runners) it runs under emulation, and the upstream README's \"first responsive request within\n~3-5 minutes\" does not hold — observed startup on an emulated arm64 host is materially slower. So the compose\nfile sets `platform: linux/amd64` **explicitly** (making behaviour deterministic instead of dependent on the\nhost's default resolution), and the readiness probe's timeout is generous and overridable by env var rather\nthan a hardcoded few minutes. The README records that a native-amd64 runner is strongly preferred.\n\n**Stated limitation, deliberately accepted:** 8.17.1 is older than the version a client such as CMS is likely\nrunning, so this harness proves REST v2 protocol semantics, not deployment-specific behavior (workflow names,\ncustom fields, permission schemes, user directories). Closing that gap requires access to the real instance and\nis tracked separately as ticket `9895-ac7d-4276-4f08`; this story's README records the exact image tag so that\ncomparison has a baseline.\n\nAdd the harness under **`tests/external/live_jira_dc/`** with a compose file, a readiness probe that polls until\nthe instance answers REST, and a fixture that provisions a scratch project and tears down everything it created.\n\n**The tier is `external`, NOT `integration` — corrected against the CI workflow.** An earlier draft marked these\n`integration`, reasoning that `make test` runs `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"`\n(**`Makefile:178`** — verified; an earlier draft cited 154, which is inside the `actionlint-bin` recipe) and so\nwould exclude them. That reasoning is wrong, and dangerously so: `_build-and-test.yml` runs\n**`pytest -m integration` as its own dedicated CI step on every commit** (\"Run the integration tier\n(concurrency + reconciler; network-guarded)\"), explicitly because those tests \"need no live services (mocked\nacli / local git)\". So the `integration` marker is an **INCLUSION into CI**, not an exclusion — Docker-requiring\ntests marked that way would be collected and run on every commit against a runner with no container, no Jira\nand no egress, breaking CI for everyone.\n\nThe `external` tier is the one genuinely fenced off, and it is enforced structurally rather than by convention:\n`tests/conftest.py::pytest_collection_modifyitems` (a) **auto-applies** the `external` marker to every item\nunder `tests/external/`, so no per-file marker is needed, and (b) **hard-fails collection** if any item carries\n`external` while living outside `tests/external/` — so a live test cannot hide elsewhere in the tree.\n`external` runs only from `external-integration.yml` (`workflow_dispatch` + a weekly cron), never on a commit.\nLiving under `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` therefore satisfies the gating requirement AND supplies the\nstructural backstop for free.\n\n**Living under `tests/external/` means INHERITING that tier's contract — three mechanisms, all enumerated\nhere because each silently changes how this suite behaves:**\n\n1. **`_require_external_opt_in` (autouse, `tests/external/conftest.py`) SKIPS every test under\n   `tests/external/` unless `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1`.** It is a deliberate leak-guard (bug\n   `4a48-6dd5-aef3-4c8e`): external tests must stay inert on a default invocation even when credentials happen\n   to be present. Consequence for this story: `make jira-dc-up && pytest tests/external/live_jira_dc/` **skips\n   everything** without the env var. The README must lead with the full invocation, and the readiness probe's\n   error message is only reachable in an opted-in run.\n2. **The `_live_jira_ready` sentinel drives the `jira_live` auto-marker and the all-skip canary.**\n   `tests/external/conftest.py::pytest_collection_modifyitems` applies `jira_live` to any module defining a\n   module-level `_live_jira_ready`, and `pytest_sessionfinish` **fails the session** when at least one\n   `jira_live` test was collected but none executed — the guard against a scheduled run that validated nothing.\n   Every existing live-Jira module defines it (`test_reconcile_live.py`, `test_link_sync_live.py`,\n   `test_link_sync_roundtrip_live.py`, `test_verified_fake_contract_live.py`). **`test_harness_smoke.py` MUST\n   define `_live_jira_ready` too**, or it opts out of the canary and a silently-all-skipped harness run would\n   look green.\n3. **`_allow_live_model_requests`** re-enables pydantic-ai's global kill-switch for this tier. Irrelevant to\n   this harness (it makes no model calls) but noted so the inherited fixture set is fully accounted for.\n\n**Skip-vs-fail is settled here, because the tier forces it.** An earlier draft's \"failure path\" said the suite\nshould FAIL with a \"harness not running\" message when the instance is down. That is wrong for this tier and\nwould break the existing `external-integration.yml` job, which already runs `pytest -m external tests/external`\nwith no Docker anywhere. The correct contract: **absent harness ⇒ SKIP with a clear, actionable message**\n(naming `make jira-dc-up` and `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1`), and the **canary** — not a hard failure — is what\nprevents a vacuous green, because a run that collected `jira_live` tests and executed none fails the session.\nThat gives both properties: no CI breakage when Docker is absent, and no silent nothing-ran success.\n\nTeardown follows **`docs/adr/0037-reconciler-live-validation-harness.md` §3** (named in full because FIVE ADRs\nshare the `0037-` prefix — code-review-novelty-convergence, cross-ticket-overlap, oss-contribution-intake-posture,\nreconciler-live-validation-harness, transport-retry — so a bare \"ADR 0037\" is unresolvable), which\n**prohibits post-deletion search-index assertions** because Jira's index\nlags creates and deletes by an unbounded interval and can return stale results either way. So the teardown\noracle is not \"query the project and find it empty\": it asserts each delete call's HTTP status and then polls\neach issue's **direct REST endpoint** (`/rest/api/2/issue/{key}`) until it returns 404, which is index-independent.\n\n**Alternative rejected: fixtures and a stateful fake alone.** rebar's test-design standard allows a verified\nfake for external-service mapping, but a fake verified only against fixtures we recorded cannot falsify\nassumptions we never thought to record — transitions, permission errors, link direction. The fake is still\nbuilt (J6 uses it for fast unit tests); this story gives it something real to be verified against.\n\n**Alternative rejected: reusing pycontribs/jira's test assertions as the oracle.** Their tests assert their\nclient's behavior, not rebar's field mapping. The reusable asset is the image and harness pattern, which is\nwhat this story takes.\n\n**Alternative rejected: the Atlassian SDK's `atlas-run-standalone`.** It also yields a licensed local Jira, but\nit needs the full SDK toolchain and its licenses are short-lived (3-day timebombs requiring `atlas-mvn clean`\nrecycling), which is hostile to repeatable CI. A pinned Docker image has neither problem.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/Dockerfile` (new) — the vendored wrapper: `FROM` the addono base **by digest**, the\n  `-DskipAllPrompts=true` entrypoint, and the three rationale comments above plus attribution to\n  pycontribs/jira.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/docker-compose.yml`, `conftest.py`, `README.md` (new) — compose `build`s the local\n  Dockerfile (it does NOT reference an unpullable image), sets `platform: linux/amd64`, `tty: true`,\n  `stdin_open: true`, mounts a persistent Maven cache volume, and publishes\n  **`127.0.0.1:2990:2990`** — loopback-scoped, NOT the bare `2990:2990` an earlier draft said, which Docker\n  binds on `0.0.0.0` and would expose a well-known-`admin`/`admin` Jira to the whole network.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_harness_smoke.py` (new).\n- **`.github/workflows/external-integration.yml` — a NEW, SEPARATE job, modelled on the `langfuse-trace` job\n  that already exists in this same file.** This is the key practical finding: the workflow ALREADY implements\n  this exact shape at lines ~159-251 for an ephemeral self-hosted Langfuse — `docker compose ... up -d`, poll\n  until healthy with an `::error::` + `logs --tail` on timeout, run the tests, dump logs `if: failure()`, and\n  `docker compose ... down -v` under `if: always()`. So the Jira job is a direct adaptation of a working\n  in-repo precedent rather than novel CI engineering; copy its structure and swap the compose file, the health\n  URL (`/jira/rest/api/2/serverInfo`) and the test path.\n\n  **`ubuntu-latest` resolves the platform problem entirely.** GitHub's `ubuntu-latest` runners are native\n  **x86_64**, so the amd64 base image runs WITHOUT emulation — which was the sole reason this harness could not\n  be verified on an Apple-Silicon workstation. rebar is a PUBLIC repo, so the standard runner is 4 vCPU /\n  16 GB, comfortably above Jira 8.17.1's needs. And because this workflow triggers only on\n  `workflow_dispatch` + a weekly cron, a 10-20 minute harness never touches per-commit CI.\n  **CI — specifically a new job in this workflow — is therefore the PRIMARY intended home for this harness's\n  acceptance evidence**, and a local run is the optional convenience path, not the reverse. This workflow is an unenumerated\n  consumer in earlier drafts: at line 141 it already runs `pytest -m external tests/external -q`, so the new\n  directory is collected by it the moment the files land, and it has **no** docker build/up/down steps. It also\n  gates its existing job on an LLM API key, which this harness does not need. So the harness gets its **own\n  job** (not an extension of the LLM-keyed one) that: builds the vendored Dockerfile, brings the instance up,\n  waits on the readiness probe, runs ONLY `tests/external/live_jira_dc/`, and tears down in an `if: always()`\n  step. It must run on a **native amd64 runner** (see the platform note above). The pre-existing job is left\n  alone, and because the harness tests skip cleanly without `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1` plus a reachable instance,\n  its collection of them stays harmless.\n- `Makefile` — a `jira-dc-up` target only; the `test:` target is NOT modified (the `integration` marker is\n  already excluded by its existing `-m` expression at **`Makefile:178`**), and `jira-dc-up` is added to the\n  `.PHONY` list at `Makefile:30` — without that, a file or directory named `jira-dc-up` would make the target\n  silently no-op.\n\n## Testing\n\nReal-external-service tier by construction — this story *is* the harness. Its own oracle is that the harness\nstarts, is usable, and provably cleans up.\n\n- happy path: the harness starts, the readiness probe reports REST answering, and a smoke test creates and\n  reads back one issue through raw REST v2.\n- auth path: a Personal Access Token minted on the 8.17.1 instance authenticates a request via\n  `Authorization: Bearer`, proving the DC auth mode the transport will use is exercised, not assumed.\n- teardown oracle (index-independent, per ADR 0037 §3): after a deliberately failing test, every issue the\n  fixture created returns 404 from its direct `/rest/api/2/issue/{key}` endpoint, and each delete call's HTTP\n  status was 2xx. Search is never consulted.\n- gating oracle: `pytest --collect-only -m \"not integration and not external\"` collects zero tests from\n  `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` — and, separately, the `external` marker is auto-applied by\n  `tests/external/conftest.py` rather than written per-file, with misplacement hard-failing collection.\n- canary participation: `test_harness_smoke.py` defines `_live_jira_ready`, so its tests carry `jira_live` and\n  an opted-in run in which every one of them skipped **fails the session** instead of reporting a vacuous pass.\n- failure path (absent harness): with the instance down, the suite **SKIPS** with a message naming\n  `make jira-dc-up` and `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1` — not a raw connection traceback, and not a hard failure, which\n  would break the pre-existing `external` CI job that has no Docker.\n\n**The `allow_insecure` obligation is NOT this story's — it belongs to J6/J7.** A draft of this plan added an AC\nrequiring every live test here to set `allow_insecure = true` and assert the cleartext warning. That was wrong\non two counts, recorded so nobody restores it: (a) the `allow_insecure` config key and its warning-emitting\nvalidator DO NOT EXIST yet — they are **J6's** deliverable ([rebar:9fd4-a94c-156e-4a56], which already carries\nexactly that AC), so J5 could not satisfy it while J6 is open, contradicting this story's whole point of being\nindependently claimable; and (b) it does not even apply here — J5's smoke tests speak **raw REST v2** to the\ninstance and never construct rebar's DC backend or read rebar's config, so no validator is on their path. The\nepic's step-7 obligation attaches to the stories whose live tests drive rebar's DC transport against this\nharness (J6/J7), which is where it is already recorded.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] the harness **builds from the vendored `tests/external/live_jira_dc/Dockerfile`** and starts (via the compose\n      file or the `jira-dc-up` target), and the readiness probe reports REST v2 answering at\n      `http://localhost:2990/jira`. The criterion is explicitly BUILD-and-start, not pull-and-start: no\n      acceptance step may depend on `pycontribs/jira-test-image` being pullable, because it is not published\n- [ ] the vendored `Dockerfile` pins the addono base **by digest** (not by tag), keeps\n      `-DskipAllPrompts=true` on the `atlas-run` entrypoint, and carries a comment explaining that removing\n      that flag breaks every cold start via the retired Marketplace v1 endpoint\n- [ ] the compose service sets `platform: linux/amd64`, `tty: true` and `stdin_open: true`, and the readiness\n      timeout is env-overridable — so an emulated arm64 host is slow rather than spuriously failing\n- [ ] a smoke test creates and reads back an issue via REST v2, and a PAT-authenticated request succeeds\n- [ ] `test_harness_smoke.py` defines a module-level `_live_jira_ready` sentinel, so its tests receive the\n      `jira_live` marker from `tests/external/conftest.py` and participate in the all-skip canary — an opted-in\n      run where every harness test skipped must FAIL the session rather than report a vacuous pass\n- [ ] with no instance running, the suite SKIPS with a message naming BOTH `make jira-dc-up` and\n      `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1` (the tier's autouse opt-in guard), and does NOT fail — verified by running the\n      directory with the harness down, which must not break the pre-existing `external` CI job\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/external-integration.yml` gains a SEPARATE amd64 job that builds the Dockerfile,\n      waits on the readiness probe, runs only `tests/external/live_jira_dc/`, and tears down under\n      `if: always()`; the pre-existing LLM-keyed job is unmodified\n- [ ] the vendored Dockerfile's entrypoint carries **`-Dproduct.start.timeout`** (empirically the correct AMPS\n      property — `-DstartupTimeout` and `-Dcargo.timeout` were verified to have NO effect) with a comment citing\n      the failure it prevents: without it Cargo's default 600000 ms deploy ceiling kills the container ~25\n      minutes in with `Deployable [...] failed to finish deploying within the timeout period [600000]`, which\n      reads like a network fault and invites a maintainer to delete the flag\n- [ ] after an intentionally failing test run, every created issue returns 404 from its direct issue endpoint\n      (no search query used anywhere in teardown verification)\n- [ ] `pytest --collect-only -m \"not integration and not external\"` reports 0 tests collected from\n      `tests/external/live_jira_dc/`, and `git diff Makefile` shows no change to the `test:` target\n- [ ] the PAT is created **programmatically** by the fixture via `POST /rest/pat/latest/tokens` authenticated\n      with the admin basic credentials (the Jira DC 8.14+ endpoint), and the resulting token is then used in an\n      `Authorization: Bearer` request — so the auth AC is self-contained and does not depend on a\n      hand-minted token\n- [ ] the readiness probe has a **stated default timeout of 20 minutes** with a 5-second poll cadence,\n      overridable by env var, and on expiry it fails with an explicit \"harness not running / not ready\" message\n      naming the compose target — never a raw connection traceback. The generous default is deliberate: an\n      emulated amd64 base plus a ~900-artifact Maven download makes upstream's quoted 3-5 minutes unattainable\n      on an arm64 runner\n- [ ] `jira-dc-up` brings up a **fresh** instance (`--force-recreate`, and the fixture refuses to run against a\n      container it did not start into a known-clean state), so an interrupted previous teardown cannot leak\n      stale Jira state into the next run\n- [ ] the compose port binding is **loopback-scoped (`127.0.0.1:2990:2990`)**, not `0.0.0.0` — this instance has\n      well-known `admin`/`admin` credentials and must not be reachable from other hosts on the network\n- [ ] the `integration`-marker exclusion has a **structural backstop**, mirroring how the `external` tier is\n      already protected: a test asserts every test module under `tests/external/live_jira_dc/` carries the marker, so a\n      future file added without it cannot silently join the default `make test` run\n- [ ] the feasibility evidence is **recorded reproducibly** in the README — the base digest, the resolved image\n      size, the host architecture it was verified on, and the observed startup phases — rather than asserted as\n      a one-off local success\n- [ ] `tests/external/live_jira_dc/README.md` records the pinned base digest and Jira version, states the\n      version-drift limitation naming ticket `9895-ac7d-4276-4f08` as where real-instance validation is\n      tracked, AND records the two operational constraints a reader would otherwise rediscover the hard way:\n      that only Jira 8.x works on this base (9+/11+ fail Maven resolution), and that the base is amd64 so an\n      arm64 host emulates it and starts materially slower than the upstream's quoted 3-5 minutes"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785368893191078001,"uuid":"b710a0c0-5d50-4e0e-88bf-052778e7cec5"}